        <blockquote>
<p dir="auto">Isn't a good thing to leave it as the source?</p>
</blockquote>
<p dir="auto">Leaving it variable leaves it the same as the source always (subject to the framerate cap, if you specify one).</p>
<blockquote>
<p dir="auto">I did not put them here, at least not now. If they are not standard, it could be a remnant several years old that I forgot about, but I doubt it: I used a standard preset without any change.</p>
</blockquote>
<p dir="auto">I'd remove it all, although I don't think much of it (other than maybe rect) is making a difference on CPU utilization.</p>
<blockquote>
<p dir="auto">I'm chasing the best overall speed for a given quality. CPU utilization seemed slow compared to my older platform, but that's not the goal, just an indicator that the CPU might not be used in the optimum way.</p>
</blockquote>
<p dir="auto">The primary control for quality is the quality target (RF).</p>
<p dir="auto">IMO there's only two x265 presets that make any sense: veryfast or slow. The others are poor tradeoffs.</p>
<p dir="auto">The comparisons to the other platform with heterogenous cores that lack SMT is misguided and misleading.</p>
<p dir="auto">If the utilization really bothers you, you could disable SMT, since the video encoders are well optimized and a bunch of additional threads with no additional execution resources behind them typically doesn't help much.</p>
    </td>
  </tr>

    </tbody>
  </table>
</task-lists>

